{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787774724,"nonce":"eOUKwdF8tXQns/JVACWXMPoEGdc=","title":"fix(web-ui): two run-view hotfixes — log skeleton flicker + ref dropdown stacking","message":"\u003e Imported from pull request #47 (MERGED)\n\u003e Branch: `hotfix/run-detail-logs-skeleton-flicker` → `main`\n\u003e Created: 2026-07-20T23:10:15Z · Merged: 2026-07-20T23:18:12Z\n\nTwo small, independent web-ui hotfixes for the run views.\n\n---\n\n\n**Problem:** On the Run detail page, while a step is *running*, the logs area keeps flashing its loading skeleton every ~second. It settles only once the run reaches a terminal state.\n\n**Root cause** (Solid.js reactivity, `StepPane.tsx`):\n1. `RunDetail.tsx` polls run status every `POLL_MS = 1200`ms while live and calls `setRun(status)`, so `selectedStep()` hands `StepPane` a **new `props.step` object every 1.2s**.\n2. The `switching` pulse effect read `stepId()` / `scoped()?.id`, which reactively subscribe to that object. Solid re-ran the effect on **every poll** — even when the id/attempt/tab were unchanged.\n3. Each re-run flipped `switching` on for 450ms, and the log body is gated `\u003cShow when={!switching()} fallback={\u003cLogSkeleton/\u003e}\u003e`. So the skeleton reappeared for 450ms out of every 1200ms → constant flicker. Polling stops at terminal state, which is why it only happened while running.\n\n**Fix:** Derive the selection identity (`step · attempt · tab`) as a `createMemo` string and drive the pulse with `on(selKey, …)`. A memo only notifies when its value actually changes, so a poll that doesn't change the selection no longer retriggers the skeleton. Genuine step/attempt/tab switches still pulse.\n\n---\n\n\n**Problem:** On the Run pipeline page, the branch/ref search dropdown went *behind* the pipelines container instead of floating on top.\n\n**Root cause** (CSS stacking, `styles.css`): `.page \u003e *` stamps every direct page child with `z-index: 1`, making each panel its own stacking context. That trapped the ref combobox dropdown (`.rp-combo-menu`, `z-index: 20`) inside the ref panel's context — it could no longer rise above the sibling pipelines panel, which (also `z-index: 1`, opaque background) won on tree order and painted over it.\n\n**Fix:** Lift `.panel-combo` above its siblings (`z-index: 5`) so the spilling dropdown floats on top.\n\n---\n\n\n- **#1:** `npm run typecheck` (tsc --noEmit) passes clean.\n- **#2:** Reproduced and fix-verified in a browser against a faithful copy of the real DOM/CSS (dropdown clipped behind the panel before; full option list floating on top after).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","files":null}]}